---
title: Nested dispatch
description: Calling the mediator from inside a handler with CreateScope() — isolated items, inherited cancellation, no ambient state to restore.
---

A handler often needs to mediate further messages: reserve stock while placing an order,
publish an event after a command succeeds. Ergosfare handles this with an explicit child
scope rather than ambient state.

```csharp
public async ValueTask HandleAsync(PlaceOrder msg, ErgosfareContext ctx)
{
    using var scope = ctx.CreateScope();   // struct scope — allocates nothing

    var reserved = await _commands.SendAsync(new ReserveStock(msg.OrderId), scope.Context);
    await _events.PublishAsync(new OrderPlaced(msg.OrderId), scope.Context);
}
```

## What the child gets

**Clean items.** The child starts with an empty item bag. Anything an inner interceptor
stashes stays inner — it cannot leak into the outer dispatch and confuse an outer
post-interceptor.

**The parent's cancellation token.** Inherited, not re-derived. Nested work cannot escape the
outer cancellation chain, and there is no token parameter to forget.

**Its own pipeline.** The inner message runs its own plan: its own interceptors, its own
handlers, its own abort semantics.

## Passing the child

Every mediator facade has an overload taking an `ErgosfareContext`:

```csharp
ValueTask        SendAsync(ICommand command, ErgosfareContext context, CommandMediationSettings? settings = null);
ValueTask<T>     SendAsync<T>(ICommand<T> command, ErgosfareContext context, CommandMediationSettings? settings = null);
ValueTask<T>     QueryAsync<T>(IQuery<T> query, ErgosfareContext context, QueryMediationSettings? settings = null);
ValueTask        PublishAsync(IEvent @event, ErgosfareContext context, EventMediationSettings? settings = null);
```

Pass `scope.Context`, not `ctx`. Passing the parent would run the inner dispatch on the
outer context and defeat the isolation.

## Abort crosses the boundary — unless you catch it

An `Abort()` inside the child cuts the **inner** pipeline and surfaces at the inner call site
as `ExecutionAbortedException`. The inner call site is your outer handler, so the outer
handler decides: catch it and carry on, or let it end the outer pipeline too.

```csharp
public async ValueTask<bool> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder msg, ErgosfareContext ctx)
{
    using var reserve = ctx.CreateScope();

    try
    {
        await _commands.SendAsync(new ReserveStock(msg.OrderId), reserve.Context);
    }
    catch (ExecutionAbortedException aborted)
    {
        _log.LogInformation("stock refused: {Reason}", aborted.Reason);
        return false;                 // the outer pipeline continues, by our choice
    }

    using var notify = ctx.CreateScope();
    await _events.PublishAsync(new OrderPlaced(msg.OrderId), notify.Context);
    return true;
}
```

Leave the `try` out and the abort keeps travelling: out of the outer handler, out of the outer
pipeline, to whoever called *that*. Nothing in between swallows it, so an inner abort ending
the whole chain is the default, and containing it is the deliberate act.

{% aside type="note" %}
Before v2.3 an inner abort ended the inner pipeline quietly and the outer handler resumed on
the next line. Nested code written against that behaviour needs the `try` added — see
[Migrating from v2.2 to v2.3](/ergosfare.docs/preview/migration/v2.2-to-v2.3).
{% /aside %}

This is the part ambient-state mediators cannot offer safely. With an `AsyncLocal` context,
an inner dispatch either overwrites the ambient value — requiring a restore step that a
`finally` block has to get exactly right — or shares state with the outer pipeline. Here
there is nothing ambient to overwrite, so there is nothing to restore.

## Parallel inner dispatches

Separate scopes are independent, so fanning out is safe:

```csharp
public async ValueTask HandleAsync(RebuildAll msg, ErgosfareContext ctx)
{
    await Task.WhenAll(msg.TenantIds.Select(async id =>
    {
        using var scope = ctx.CreateScope();
        await _commands.SendAsync(new RebuildTenant(id), scope.Context);
    }));
}
```

Each iteration rents its own child context. They share the parent's cancellation token and
nothing else.

{% aside type="caution" %}
Do not share one scope across concurrent inner dispatches. One scope means one context, and
a context is single-dispatch state.
{% /aside %}

## Lifetime

`ErgosfareContextScope` is a `readonly struct` implementing `IDisposable`, and `using`
returns the pooled child. Its constructor is internal, so `CreateScope()` is the only way to
get one — a scope always has a parent. As with any pooled context, the child **must not be
used after the scope is disposed** — see
[Execution context](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/execution-context#pooling-and-the-lifetime-rule).

```csharp
// Wrong — the child is back in the pool by the time this runs.
ErgosfareContext leaked;
using (var scope = ctx.CreateScope())
    leaked = scope.Context;

leaked.Set("oops", true);
```

